{"id":"neet-2026-q031","pn":31,"y":"NEET 2026 · 21 Jun · Re-exam","tp":"Electromagnetic Induction","st":"Induced emf and power in a loop","q":"A conducting loop of finite resistance lies on the x − y plane. There is a constant magnetic field in the z direction. The area of the loop varies with time t, as A = A₀(1 + sin t) in appropriate units. The figure that correctly indicates the qualitative behaviour of the power P dissipated in the loop as a function of time is:","o":["Graph (1): P starts at a non-zero value, falls in a concave-down arc to zero (cusp), then rises again","Graph (2): P is constant (horizontal line)","Graph (3): P rises smoothly and increasingly from zero","Graph (4): P shows two humps, zero at t = 0, a zero in between, and zero at the end"],"c":0,"s":"A = A₀(1 + sin t).\nFlux φ = BA = BA₀(1 + sin t).\nEmf ε = −dφ/dt = −BA₀ cos t.\nI_induced = ε/R = −(BA₀/R) cos t.\nPower P = I²R = B²A₀²cos²t/R.\nSo P ∝ cos²t: non-zero at t = 0, falls to zero, then rises again (graph 1)."},
{"id":"neet-2026-q037","pn":37,"y":"NEET 2026 · 21 Jun · Re-exam","tp":"Electromagnetic Induction","st":"Self inductance of solenoid","q":"Consider a long solenoid of length l and radius r. If n is the number of turns per unit length and μ₀ is the permeability of free space, the inductance of the solenoid is :","o":["μ₀n²r²l","(μ₀/2π)n²r²l","2μ₀πn²r²l","μ₀πn²r²l"],"c":3,"s":"Self inductance of solenoid: L = Nφ/i.\nφ = BA, so L = NBA/i.\nB = μ₀ni, so L = Nμ₀niA/i, where n = N/l.\nL = Nμ₀nA.\nL = (N/l)μ₀nAl.\nL = μ₀n²Al.\nSince A = πr², L = μ₀n²πr²l."},
{"id":"neet-2026-q042","pn":42,"y":"NEET 2026 · 21 Jun · Re-exam","tp":"Electromagnetic Induction","st":"Inductors in series and parallel","q":"Two identical inductors are connected in two different configurations P and Q, where a time varying current I(t) is flowing, as shown in the figure. The induced emf between points a and b for configuration P is EP and that for configuration Q is EQ. The ratio EP/EQ is:\n[Neglect the effect of mutual inductance.]","o":["1/2","1","2","1/4"],"c":2,"s":"In figure (P) the two inductors are in series, so the inductance between a and b is that of one inductor L.\nEP = Eab = −L dI(t)/dt.\nIn figure (Q) the two inductors are in parallel, giving L/2.\nEQ = Eab = −(L/2) dI(t)/dt.\nEP/EQ = 2/1."}
